            "text": "{\n  \"persuasion\": \"PERSUADED\",\n  \"support_scope\": \"FULL\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book's definition of a flood as 'peoples in motion' (specifically an invading army or the Lord's host) is explicitly supported by Isaiah 8:7 (which identifies overflowing waters as the king of Assyria) and Jeremiah 47:2 (which identifies a flood from the north as an army). It integrates perfectly with the broader scriptural definition that 'waters' symbolize 'peoples' (Revelation 17:15). The counter-interpretation that 'flood' simply means a generic overwhelming disaster fails to account for why the prophets explicitly equate the overflowing waters with specific invading nations, and why Revelation explicitly defines waters as peoples.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"A flood symbolizes peoples in motion, specifically an invading nation or the Lord's host sweeping an enemy away.\",\n  \"book_secondary_elaborations\": [\n    \"Destroying waters\"\n  ],\n  \"unsupported_glossary_assertions\": [],\n  \"chapter_only_objections\": [\n    \"The specific application of Daniel 9:26 to 'the Lord's own host' relies on a minority grammatical reading of the Hebrew subject and verb, which the chapter delegates to another chapter ('Daniel Unsealed') for full defense.\",\n    \"The chapter's derivation assumes that personal distress 'floods' in the Psalms are a scaled-down application of the same symbol, which may stretch the 'peoples in motion' concept when applied to individual circumstances.\"\n  ],\n  \"strongest_counter_interpretation\": \"A flood is a general, natural metaphor for overwhelming disaster, destruction, or distress. While it is frequently applied to invading armies due to their overwhelming nature, it is not strictly defined as 'peoples in motion' across all scriptural contexts.\",\n  \"counter_core_identification\": \"A general metaphor for overwhelming destruction, disaster, or distress.\",\n  \"counter_relation_to_book_core\": \"SAME_CORE_BROADER\",\n  \"material_core_denial\": \"Denies that the symbol is structurally derived from 'waters = peoples', arguing instead that the mapping to invading armies is based purely on the shared trait of being an overwhelming, unstoppable physical force.\",\n  \"counter_case\": [\n    \"Natural phenomena are often used as metaphors based on their observable physical characteristics (e.g., a literal flood is overwhelming and destructive).\",\n    \"Many poetic passages (especially in the Psalms) use flood and water imagery to describe personal distress, divine wrath, or general chaos without any reference to 'peoples' or nations.\",\n    \"The traditional reading of Daniel 9:26 ('its end shall come with a flood') treats the flood as a general figure for a catastrophic, decisive end, rather than specifically identifying it as the Messiah's army.\"\n  ],\n  \"book_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Directly explains the explicit biblical glosses that define overflowing waters as an invading king (Isaiah 8:7) and a northern army (Jeremiah 47:2).\",\n    \"Structurally connects 'flood' to the explicit definition of 'waters' as 'peoples, multitudes, and nations' given in Revelation 17:15.\",\n    \"Provides a unified systemic meaning for various water-related symbols (sea, rivers, floods, waves) based on the single foundational equation that water = people.\",\n    \"Accounts for the symmetry of the Exodus typology, where the pursuing 'flood' (Egypt) is swallowed by the sea.\"\n  ],\n  \"counter_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"More naturally accommodates poetic and individual uses of flood imagery (e.g., Jonah's distress or David's psalms of deliverance) without requiring them to be scaled-down versions of 'peoples' or nations.\",\n    \"Requires less complex grammatical maneuvering to explain passages like Daniel 9:26.\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\": [],\n  \"comparative_winner\": \"BOOK\",\n  \"decisive_sources\": [\n    \"Isaiah 8:7\",\n    \"Jeremiah 47:2\",\n    \"Revelation 17:15\"\n  ],\n  \"unresolved_objections\": [\n    \"The application of the symbol to purely personal or individual distress in the Psalms is not fully resolved by the 'peoples in motion' definition alone, though the chapter hints at how it scales.\"\n  ],\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
